  <Patch Id="ARFrlwyYUL9vlQwUyGX60e">
    <Canvas Id="CZlK1ABp28XuryvzO9y4nw" DefaultCategory="Main" CanvasType="FullCategory" />
    <Node Name="Application" Bounds="100,100" Id="MBnvagQv50gvG4UCDE67VP">
      <p:NodeReference>
        <Choice Kind="ContainerDefinition" Name="Process" />
        <FullNameCategoryReference ID="Primitive" />
      </p:NodeReference>
      <Patch Id="Cx2JejUb6oVMT0UkSyVI3o">
        <Canvas Id="A9GLAb7zt1A2mnsnJ38eA8" CanvasType="Group">
          <Pad Id="Qf9UyIMrrxZKveWvkUY9wh" Bounds="-880,-140,660,900" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="EXPLANATION - Sharper is a different question&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;The same place answers at every sharpness, and each sharpness is a different question with its own&#xD;&#xA;price. Under this map sits a PYRAMID: the whole world's elevation, stored again at every zoom&#xD;&#xA;level, so that ANY sharpness is one fetch away. This patch asks all six at once.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;FLIP THE ELEVATION TOGGLE (left, off when you arrive - opening a patch is not agreeing to network&#xD;&#xA;traffic; the BASEMAP toggle is the same deal for the map itself). Then glide the cursor. The place&#xD;&#xA;under it is asked its height six times - once per zoom 8..13, one Terrarium tile each - and the&#xD;&#xA;six answers disagree ON PURPOSE: at z8 one pixel is ~500 m of ground and the summit is averaged&#xD;&#xA;into its valleys; at z13 a pixel is ~16 m and the number is nearly the survey's. Sharper does not&#xD;&#xA;mean 'the same answer, prettier'. It is a different question about a different amount of world.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;TWO THINGS TO WATCH, both on the picture:&#xD;&#xA;  BYTES per answer FALL as sharpness rises (about 120 KB at z8 down to ~40 KB at z13 over&#xD;&#xA;  mountains) - a surprise worth having: sharpness costs tile COUNT, not tile size. Each level&#xD;&#xA;  down covers 1/4 the ground, so the whole-world bill is 4x per level; the single tile gets&#xD;&#xA;  simpler and compresses better.&#xD;&#xA;  WHICH COLUMNS RE-FETCH as you glide: the z13 column asks again every ~4 km of ground, z8 only&#xD;&#xA;  every ~130. The pyramid's cost lives at its sharp end - which is why every tile map you have&#xD;&#xA;  ever used (the basemap under this one included) fetches the sharp levels only for the window&#xD;&#xA;  you are actually looking at.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;The ladder is everywhere once you see it: mipmaps in 3D graphics, COG overviews, point-cloud&#xD;&#xA;levels of detail - the same trade, truth by degrees, priced in tiles. Chapter 'How high is here'&#xD;&#xA;has the pixel contract (Terrarium's (R*256+G+B/256)-32768); this patch reads the same pixel at&#xD;&#xA;six resolutions instead of one.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;HONEST EDGES: nearest pixel, no interpolation - a real terrain reader blends neighbours. The six&#xD;&#xA;heights come from six INDEPENDENT files that do not know about each other; the pyramid promises&#xD;&#xA;consistency, not identity. Over the sea expect ~0 at every rung. And the answers arrive a beat&#xD;&#xA;after you stop moving: each is a real HTTP fetch, gated so a column only re-asks when its own&#xD;&#xA;tile number changes.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;PLAY&#xD;&#xA;  take the ladder somewhere extreme: Everest (86.925 E 27.988 N), the Dead Sea, your street.&#xD;&#xA;  wheel the MAP's zoom in and out - the basemap is climbing the same pyramid; this patch just&#xD;&#xA;  shows the rungs side by side.&#xD;&#xA;  point at a coastline and watch the coarse columns call it sea while the sharp ones find land.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;GIS WORDS for what you just did: the stack is an IMAGE PYRAMID (its levels are OVERVIEWS);&#xD;&#xA;reading the level that matches your question is RESOLUTION SELECTION - and 'resolution is part of&#xD;&#xA;access' is the whole idea. CREATIVE CONNECTION: a mipmap is exactly this, invented for texture&#xD;&#xA;filtering in 1983.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;NEXT - three directions, no order:&#xD;&#xA;  Prompt How high is here                        one rung of this ladder, with the full contract&#xD;&#xA;  Explanation You do not have to read everything the other half of the idea: WHERE you read is&#xD;&#xA;                                                 part of the query too&#xD;&#xA;  Tutorial 09                                    what a file that arrives over the network owes you&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Data: Terrarium terrain tiles (AWS Open Data / Mapzen) - credit drawn on the picture, required by&#xD;&#xA;the terms. Basemap: OpenStreetMap, credit on the map. Both toggles start OFF.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">11</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
